## DESCRIPTION
##   Using R to find descriptive statistics
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Summary statistics)
## Date(01/01/15)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz)
## Level(2)
## KEYWORDS('Descriptive Statistics')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextUnits.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Units')->addUnits('texts' => { text => 1, aliases => ['text'] });

#This makes the connected problems have the same seed
SRAND($psvn);

@data = num_sort(map { random(5, 50) } (1 .. 5));
$sum  = 0;
$sum += $_ for (@data);

$median = $data[2];
$mean   = $sum / 5;

$outlier        = random(120, 300);
$median_outlier = ($data[2] + $data[3]) / 2;
$mean_outlier   = ($sum + $outlier) / 6;

@x = @data[ random_subset(5, 0 .. 4) ];

BEGIN_PGML
The table below gives the number of text messages five students receive on a particular day:

[@ DataTable(
  [
    [['Number of texts', headerrow => 1,noencase=>1],@x],
  ], 
  encase => ['\(','\)'],
  align => '| c | c | c| c | c |c |',
  midrules => 1
); @]*
  
*Case 1.*  For the data give above, calculate the following:

a) Compute the mean of the number of texts and include units in your answer.

    [`\bar{x} = `] [_]{Compute("$mean texts")}

b) What is [`n`] for the text data? 

    [`n = `]  [_]{5}

c) Compute the median number of texts (include units in your answer.)

    Median: [_]{Compute("$median texts")}

*Case 2.*. Suppose we included the number of texts for one more student who had [`[$outlier]`] texts that day. Add this observation to the data set above and re-compute the following.

a) The mean (include units in your answer)

    [`\bar{x} = `] [_]{Compute("$median_outlier texts")}

b) What is [`n`] for the text data now? 

    [`n = `] [_]{6}

c) Compute the median number of texts (include units in your answer.)

    Median: [_]{Compute("$median_outlier texts")}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

1. For the original data set:

    a) [`\bar{x} = [$mean]`] texts

    b) [`n = 5`]

    c) Median [`=`] [`[$median]`] texts

2. For the data set with the additional data point:

    a) [`\bar{x} = [$mean_outlier]`] texts

    b) [`n = 6`]

    c) Median [`=`] [`[$median_outlier]`] texts
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
